# L5R 5E - LEGACIES OF WAR (LORE)

**Version:** 0.4
**Source:** Legend of the Five Rings Roleplaying Game, 5th Edition - Legacies of War
**Publisher:** Fantasy Flight Games

---

## CHARACTER LEGACIES

Those who commit great deeds are remembered long after their mortal lives end, their legends lingering in the stories told of them and in the actions of their successors and descendants. Legacies are one way of connecting past PCs (predecessor PCs) to new characters (successor PCs), giving players a mechanical and narrative link between characters across a campaign's arc.

### Themes of Legacies

Legacies play to one of Legend of the Five Rings' core thematic conflicts: that everything in the mortal realm is fleeting and yet somehow mortal choices have vast meaning, on both a personal and setting-wide scale. The apparent contradiction between these themes serves to heighten each as long as both are used in roughly equal measure over the course of a story.

On the side of accepting the transience of all works, Legacies give players an incentive to accept or even encourage the fleeting nature of their characters, emboldening them to take risks that are logical to their characters' mindsets and development in the story, and to retire characters once their story arc is complete.

Legacies also emphasize the power of choice. An individual's decision to live or die in a particular way might seem fleeting in the scope of history, but it has rippling consequences, each of which indelibly marks the lives of others. Legacies are a way to communicate that these things matter beyond the extent of an individual's life, and give players a way to carry on unfinished themes and plotlines even if the original characters who started them no longer can.

### When to Use Legacies in a Campaign

Legacies are best suited to two types of campaigns: very long campaigns, and campaigns which have periodic breaks in the narrative that allow for significant amounts of time to pass. They can be used in any campaign where a player retires a character or the group wants to play other characters for a time.

### Using Legacies in Play

Each Legacy has several key elements: a **requirement** that the predecessor PC must have met for the successor PC to select the template; **types** like the ones all other advantages possess; a **charge** that represents the character's obligation to their predecessor's memory; and an **effects** category that functions like that of a distinction.

Legacies are a new kind of advantage, separate from distinctions and passions. They are applied at the same time as distinctions. Like distinctions, they can be inverted at the GM's discretion, applying as if they were an adversity.

### Creating a Legacy

Legacies are special advantages derived from the mechanical and narrative accomplishments of a past PC. Each Legacy is unique, though Legacy templates are provided to make creation quick and easy.

**Step One: Select Predecessor and Legacy Template.** Choose the character the PC is picking up the mantle from, usually the player's former PC. Note any Legacy template requirements that must be met.

**Step Two: Customize Template.** The GM and the player work together to customize the selected Legacy template, making any relevant choices.

**Step Three: Choose Name.** By default, a Legacy's name is "Legacy of [Name of Predecessor Character]," though GMs and players are encouraged to come up with creative alternatives.

### Creating Custom Legacy Templates

GMs and players can create custom Legacy templates if the provided templates do not cover their concept. The first step is to determine the crux of the predecessor's character -- specifically, in what way did their life impact the character who would be using the template. Then, choose the ring most commonly used with this ability.

Once the main narrative tie is established, determine a requirement for a predecessor to meet in order to have this as a Legacy. The charge usually follows a pattern of removing 3 strife for upholding the Legacy or gaining 3 strife and 1 Void point for failing to do so. The effects section includes a narrative boon and a dice reroll effect that must be broad enough to be used in a variety of situations but specific enough not to be constantly applicable.

